+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+Greenfield TypeScript library (ESM, strict, NodeNext, no runtime dependencies); `src/` is empty. See proposal.md. Tests run with `node --test`.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- A small, dependency-free `resolvePointer` that follows RFC 6901 strictly, in its own module re-exported by `src/index.ts`, so later features can reuse it.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- URI fragment form, mutation, and custom error classes beyond a plain `Error` subclass.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Parse, then walk.** Validate and split the whole pointer into decoded tokens first, so an invalid pointer errors regardless of the document. Alternative: validate lazily while walking; rejected because it would make validity depend on the document.
+- **Decode with `~1` before `~0`**, after checking every `~` is followed by `0` or `1`.
+- **Own-property lookup only** (`Object.hasOwn`) on objects, so `__proto__`, `constructor` etc. do not resolve through the prototype chain.
+- **Array index check by regex `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$`** and a bounds check; `-` throws since it names a nonexistent element.
+- **Errors, not `undefined`:** a JSON value can never be `undefined`, but throwing is what the request requires and is unambiguous for `null` values.
+- **Export a `JsonPointerError extends Error`** so callers can distinguish it, while still satisfying "throws an error".
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Huge array index strings] → bounds check against `length` after regex match; no numeric precision issue since out-of-range values simply fail comparison.
